  "text": "An analysis of the given equation suggests that each digit in the second number is a sequence number of the corresponding letter in the alphabet minus 1 (e.g. $C = 3 - 1 = 2$, $D = 4 - 1 = 3$, etc.). Applying the same logic to each letter, we get:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} &&A&B&C&D_b\\\\ &+&B&C&D&A_b\\\\ \\cline{2-6} &E&F&G&H&I_b\\\\ \\end{array}$$ $$\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} &&A&(\\text{A+1})&(\\text{B+1})&(\\text{C+1})&(\\text{D+1})_b\\\\ &+&B&(\\text{A+1})&(\\text{B+1})&(\\text{C+1})&(\\text{D+1})_b\\\\ \\cline{2-6} &E&(\\text{A+2})&(\\text{B+2})&(\\text{C+2})&(\\text{D+2})&(\\text{H+1})_b.\\\\ \\end{array}$$\n\nSince $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, and $H$ are all digits in the number system with base $b$, they must be between 0 and $b-1$. However, $H$ is greater than 9, as it is the result of adding eight to $D$. Therefore, $b$ must be greater than 9.\n\nFurthermore, $I$ is a single digit, so it must also be between 0 and $b-1$. However, $I = A + 2$, implying that $A + 1 + B + 1 + C + 1 + D + 1 \\leq b$. Simplifying, we get $A + B + C + D \\leq b - 4$. Since $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, and $H$ are all between 0 and 9 (excluding $H$), the maximum value for $A + B + C + D$ is 36. Therefore, $b$ must be greater than 40.\n\nLastly, since $I$ is a single digit, $b-40 < 10$. Combining these constraints, we find that the only possible value for $b$ is 41.\nThe answer is: 41",
